					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/modernmajorgeneral" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(274283); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=274283&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606834615" alt="ModernMajorGeneral" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=274283)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/modernmajorgeneral" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(274283); return false;" >ModernMajorGeneral</a></span> <span class="odate time_1535106757 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">24 Aug 2018 10:32</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3924230">
									

<blockquote>
<p>The majority of the population, however, saw no evidence of SCP-4449's claims of combating yōkai and merely assumed that SCP-4449 was some form of delusional ghost.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I have some difficulty imagining people who are prepared to acknowledge the existence of a ghost but dismiss its claims of evil spirits with a requirement for hard evidence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>As of late 2017, Higashishirakawa's infectious disease, genetic disorder, schizophrenia, major depressive disorder, suicide, cancer, and infant mortality diagnosis rates have risen to more than quintuple the national per capita average, to the point of exceeding total rates reported by towns with ten times Higashishirakawa's population.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I doubt whether the baseline absolute numbers of infant mortality or genetic disorder in a &lt;2000 population Japanese rural town are high enough such that a fivefold or even tenfold increase would be noticeable as statistically significant.</p>
<p>This is played <em>very</em> straight as a helpful spirit with a moral lesson about how removing its influence causes bad stuff to happen. This shows up so many times in media everywhere and also on the SCP wiki that I can't get excited about it. It reminds me of <a href="/scp-2788">SCP-2788</a>, but with less social/historical commentary. This was nicely executed enough that I novoted, though.</p>

							</div>

								
					
