<p>(disclaimer: not Japanese, anyone with real qualifications feel free to correct this)</p>
<p>I would agree with you if the scarecrow had appeared in some random country, but this is a spirit in Japan talking about established concepts from dominant Japanese spirituality/folklore/culture. Apart from many Japanese people already believing in yōkai anyway, the others are now faced with a spirit claiming that other spirits are also real - which their grandparents believe in, more spiritual people also believe in, they have grown up hearing stories about and continue to be exposed to various beliefs around spirits. In this situation accepting the folk traditions as a whole package makes sense to explain this supernatural occurrence.</p>
<p>It's a bit like a biblical angel appearing in a Western country, and everyone, even atheists, agreeing 'yep this is definitely an angel', but refusing to accept the angel's pronouncements about demons or Christianity. I would have a hard time justifying that even as a non-religious person myself.</p>

